The lottery ticket hypothesis is formalized as follows. Let \(f(x;\theta)\) be a deep neural network with input \(x\) and weights \(\theta\). Initial weights are \(\theta_0 \sim \mathcal{D}_\theta\). Let \(T\) be the total number of training epochs. A binary mask \(m \in \{0,1\}^{|\theta|}\) is obtained from a pruning algorithm (e.g., iterative or one-shot magnitude pruning), where \(|\theta|\) is the total number of weights and \(\|m\|\) is the number of non-zero entries. Let \(\theta_T\) be the weights after training the dense network \(f(x;\theta_0)\) for \(T\) epochs with a desirable training recipe (appropriate learning rate and sufficient epochs). Let \(\theta^{SD}\) be the weights of a small-dense network with the same number of non-zero parameters as \(\|m\|\). Let \(\theta_0' \sim \mathcal{D}_\theta\) be a random reinitialization independent of \(\theta_0\). Under a non-trivial sparsity ratio \(s\) (the percentage of pruned weights), there exists a sparse, identically-initialized subnetwork \(f(x; m \odot \theta_0)\) such that, when trained in isolation for \(T' \le T\) epochs with a decent learning rate, it attains test accuracy \(A_{\mathrm{LT}}\) satisfying:
(i) \(A_{\mathrm{LT}} \approx A_{\mathrm{PRE}}\) where \(A_{\mathrm{PRE}}\) is the accuracy of the well-trained dense network \(f(x;\theta_T)\);
(ii) \(A_{\mathrm{LT}} > A_{\mathrm{SD}}\) where \(A_{\mathrm{SD}}\) is the accuracy of the small-dense network \(f(x;\theta^{SD})\);
(iii) \(A_{\mathrm{LT}} > A_{\mathrm{RR}}\) where \(A_{\mathrm{RR}}\) is the accuracy of the same mask applied to a random reinitialization \(f(x;\theta_0' \odot m)\).
The numeric thresholds for "similar accuracy" (\(\approx\)) depend on the dataset: for CIFAR-10, the accuracy drop \(\Delta = A_{\mathrm{PRE}} - A_{\mathrm{LT}}\) must satisfy \(\Delta \le 0.5\%\); for CIFAR-100 and Tiny-ImageNet, \(\Delta \le 1\%\); for ImageNet-1K, \(\Delta \le 1.5\%\). A "clear gap" between \(f(x; (\theta_0 \odot m)_T)\) (the subnetwork trained from original initialization for \(T\) epochs) and \(f(x; (\theta_0' \odot m)_T)\) (the subnetwork trained from random reinitialization for \(T\) epochs) is defined as an accuracy difference greater than \(0.5\%\).

The rigorous definition of the lottery ticket hypothesis specifies five conditions for a winning ticket:
(1) non-trivial sparsity ratio \(s\) (exceeding approximately 60%) and sufficient training epochs \(T\) (e.g., 160 for CIFAR-10/100 and Tiny-ImageNet, 90 for ImageNet-1K);
(2) the accuracy of a small-dense network with the same number of nonzero parameters as the subnetwork is clearly lower than the subnetwork accuracy;
(3) there exists a learning rate such that the subnetwork trained from original initialization \(f(x; (\theta_0 \odot m)_T)\) achieves an accuracy at least \(0.5\%\) higher than the subnetwork trained from random reinitialization \(f(x; (\theta_0' \odot m)_T)\) trained with any learning rate;
(4) there exists a learning rate such that the subnetwork accuracy is similar to the pretrained dense network accuracy at that same learning rate, where similarity is defined as accuracy drop \(\le 0.5\%\) for CIFAR-10, \(\le 1\%\) for CIFAR-100 and Tiny-ImageNet, \(\le 1.5\%\) for ImageNet-1K;
(5) there exists a learning rate such that the subnetwork accuracy is similar to the well-trained original network (trained with a desirable recipe, e.g., initial learning rate that yields highest accuracy) according to the same dataset-dependent thresholds.
A subnetwork satisfying all five conditions is a "Jackpot winning ticket"; one satisfying (1)–(4) but not (5) is a "secondary prize ticket". Evaluating architectures (ResNet-20, ResNet-32, ResNet-18, ResNet-50, VGG-16, MobileNet-v1) on CIFAR-10/100, Tiny-ImageNet, and ImageNet-1K using one-shot magnitude pruning (OMP) and iterative magnitude pruning (IMP) (IMP prunes 20% of remaining weights per iteration; OMP prunes all weights to target sparsity in one step) at the best-suited learning rates, no Jackpot winning tickets are found within the evaluated combinations. Secondary prize tickets are commonly found on smaller datasets (CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100) with appropriately sized networks.
